[Figure: A geometric diagram of quadrilateral ABCD with interior points E, F, G, H forming an inscribed quadrilateral, connected by crossing diagonals.]

Let \(ABCD\) be the Tetragon; and let the 3 sides, \(AB\), \(BC\), \(CD\), be bisected by vertices of the Parallelogram \(EFGH\).

Join \(BD\).

\(\because\), in Triangle \(BCD\), sides \(BC\), \(CD\) are bisected at \(F\) and \(G\),

\(\therefore FG\) is parallel to \(BD\);

but \(EH\) is parallel to \(FG\);

\(\therefore EH\) is parallel to \(BD\);

\(\therefore\) Triangles \(AEH\), \(ABD\) are similar;

now \(AE\) is half of \(AB\);

\(\therefore AH\) is half of \(AD\).

Q. E. D.

[Figure: A geometric diagram of triangle ABC with an inscribed triangle A'B'C' and central point O, connected by multiple cevians.]

Let \(ABC\) be the given Triangle, and \(A'B'C'\) the required Triangle, so that \(\angle BA'C' = \angle CA'B'\), &c.

Evidently \(A'C'\), \(A'B'\) are equally inclined to a line drawn, from \(A'\), \(\perp BC\); and so of the others: i. e. these \(\perp\)s bisect the \(\angle\)s at \(A'\), \(B'\), \(C'\);

\(\therefore\) they meet in the same Point. Draw them; let them meet at \(O\); and call the \(\angle C'A'B'\) '\(2\alpha\)', and so on.

Now \((\beta + \gamma) = \pi - \angle B'OC' = A\);

\(\therefore 2A = 2(\beta + \gamma) = \pi - 2\alpha\);

\(\therefore \alpha = 90°-A\);

\(\therefore \angle BA'C'= A\).

Similarly, \(\angle BC'A'= C\).

\(\therefore\) Triangle \(BC'A'\) is similar to Triangle \(BCA\); and so of the others; \[ \begin{aligned} \therefore BA' & = \frac{c}{a} \cdot BC' = \frac{c}{a} \cdot (c - AC'),\\ & = \frac{c}{a} \cdot (c - \frac{b}{c} \cdot AB'),\\ & = \frac{c^{2}}{a} - \frac{b}{a} \cdot (b - CB'),\\ & = \frac{c^{2}}{a} - \frac{b^{2}}{a} + \frac{b}{a} \cdot \frac{a}{b} \cdot CA',\\ & = \frac{c^{2}}{a} - \frac{b^{2}}{a} + a - BA';\\ \therefore 2BA' & = \frac{c^{2}+a^{2}-b^{2}}{a} = \frac{2ca\cos {B}}{a};\\ \therefore BA' & = c\cos {B}; \end{aligned} \]

\(\therefore A'\) is foot of \(\perp\) drawn, from \(A\), to \(BC\). Hence the construction is obvious.

Q. E. F.

Let \(AB\), \(AC\), be the given Lines, and \(P\) the given Point; and join \(AP\).

[Figure: Parallelogram AEGF (left) and parallelogram AKPH (right) overlap near center points G, K, H, M. Line AC runs horizontally through base; line AB crosses diagonally upper right through point L and P, with interior intersection points labeled K, H, M.]

Through \(A\) draw \(EAF\), \(\perp AP\), and bisected at \(A\); from \(E\), \(F\), draw \(EG\), \(FH\), parallel to \(AP\), and meeting \(AB\), \(AC\), at \(G\), \(H\); join \(GH\), and on it describe a semicircle cutting \(AP\) at \(K\); and join \(KG\), \(KH\). Then \(\angle GKH\) is a right angle. From \(P\) draw \(PL\), \(PM\), parallel to \(KG\), \(KH\).

Now Triangle \(APL\) has, to Triangle \(AKG\), the duplicate ratio of \(AP\) to \(AK\);

but so also has triangle \(APM\) to Triangle \(AKH\);

also Triangles \(AKG\), \(AKH\), are equal, being on the same base \(AK\), and having equal altitudes \(AE\), \(AF\);

\(\therefore\) Triangles \(APL\), \(APM\) are equal: and \(\angle LPM\) is evidently equal to \(\angle GKH\); \(\therefore\) it is a right angle.

Q. E. F.

[Figure: A triangle with vertices A (top), B (bottom left), and C (bottom right). A vertical altitude is drawn from apex A perpendicular to base BC, with the altitude labeled α, dividing the base into two segments.]

Call given altitudes '\(\alpha\), \(\beta\), \(\gamma\)'.

Now \(a\alpha = b\beta = c\gamma\);

\(\therefore \alpha\sin A = \beta\sin B = \gamma\sin C\);

\(\therefore \dfrac{\sin A}{\beta\gamma} = \dfrac{\sin B}{\gamma\alpha} = \dfrac{\sin C}{\alpha\beta} = k\) (say);

\(\therefore \sin A = k\beta\gamma,\quad \sin B = k\gamma\alpha,\quad \sin C = k\alpha\beta\).

Now \(\sin (A+B) = \sin C\);

\(\therefore \sin A\cos B + \cos A\sin B = \sin C\);

\(\therefore \sin A\cos B = \sin C - \cos A\sin B\);

\(\therefore \sin ^{2}A (1-\sin ^{2}B) = \sin ^{2}C + \sin ^{2}B (1-\sin ^{2}A) -2\sin C\cos A\sin B\);

\(\therefore \sin ^{2}A -\sin ^{2}A\sin ^{2}B = \sin ^{2}C+ \sin ^{2}B - \sin ^{2}A\sin ^{2}B -2\sin B\sin C\cos A\);

\(\therefore \sin ^{2}A -\sin ^{2}B -\sin ^{2}C = -2\sin B\sin C\cos A\);

\(\therefore, \text{squaring},\quad (\sin ^{4}A + {\&c.}) -2\sin ^{2}A\sin ^{2}B -2\sin ^{2}A\sin ^{2}C +2\sin ^{2}B\sin ^{2}C = 4\sin ^{2}B\sin ^{2}C(1-\sin ^{2}A)\);

\(\therefore (\sin ^{4}A + {\&c.}) - 2(\sin ^{2}B\sin ^{2}C + {\&c.}) +4\sin ^{2}A\sin ^{2}B\sin ^{2}C =0\);

\(\therefore\), substituting for \(\sin A\), &c., and dividing by \(k^{4}\), \((\beta^{4}\gamma^{4} + {\&c.}) -2\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}\gamma^{2}.(\alpha^{2} + {\&c.}) +4k^{2}\alpha^{4}\beta^{4}\gamma^{4} =0\);

[Figure: Triangle ABC with altitude from A to base BC labeled α.]

\(\therefore \quad k^{2} = \dfrac{2\alpha^{2}\beta^{2}\gamma^{2}(\alpha^{2} + \&c.) - (\beta^{4}\gamma^{4} + {\&c.})}{4\alpha^{4}\beta^{4}\gamma^{4}}\).

Now \(\sin {A} = k\beta\gamma\), &c.; which answers (2).

Also \(\alpha = b\sin {C}\); and similarly \(\gamma = a\sin {B}\);

\(\therefore \quad a = \dfrac{\gamma}{\sin {B}} = \dfrac{\gamma}{k\gamma\alpha} = \dfrac{1}{k\alpha}\), \text{&c.}; which answers (1).

Also area \(= \dfrac{bc\sin {A}}{2} = \dfrac{1}{2} \cdot \dfrac{1}{k\beta} \cdot \dfrac{1}{k\gamma} \cdot k\beta\gamma = \dfrac{1}{2k}\);

which answers (3).

Q.E.F.

(*Analysis.*)

Let \(ABC\) be the given Triangle, and \(A'B'C'\) the required one; and let the ratio, which \(B'C'\) has to \(BC\), be '\(k\)'; so that \(k\) is less than 1.

Since \(BB'=CC'\), and that \(BC\), \(B'C'\), are parallel, it may easily be proved, by dropping perpendiculars from \(B'\), \(C'\), upon \(BC\), which must necessarily be equal, that \(\angle\text{s}\) \(B'BC\), \(C'CB\), are equal.

Similarly, \(\angle\text{s}\) \(A'AC\), \(C'CA\), are equal; and so are \(\angle\text{s}\) \(A'AB\), \(B'BA\).

[Figure: A triangle with vertices A (top), B (bottom left), C (bottom right). Interior triangle A'B'C' is inscribed with A' (upper middle), B' (lower left), C' (lower right). Point D lies at the center of A'B'C', with cevians from each outer vertex through the inner triangle converging near D.]

Call \(\angle B'BC\) '\(\theta\)'; then \(\angle C'CB=\theta\);

\(\therefore\angle C'CA=C-\theta=\angle A'AC\);

\(\therefore\angle A'AB=A-(C-\theta)=\angle B'BA\).

Now \(\angle\text{s}~B'BC,~B'BA\), together \(=B\);

\(\therefore\theta+A-(C-\theta)=B\);

\(\therefore 2\theta=B+C-A=180°-2A\);

\(\therefore\theta=90°-A\).

Hence, if \(BB'\), \(CC'\), be produced to meet at \(D\), Triangle \(DBC\) will be isosceles, with a vertical \(\angle\) equal to \(2A\).

Now, if a Circle be drawn about \(ABC\), and its centre joined to \(B\) and \(C\), the Triangle, so formed, will fulfil the same conditions;

hence the centre of this Circle will be \(D\);

hence the construction.

(*Synthesis.*)

Bisect the sides, and draw perpendiculars, meeting at \(D\). Join \(D\) to the vertices \(B\), \(C\). From \(DB\) cut off \(DB'= k.DB\). From \(B'\) draw \(B'C'\) parallel to \(BC\).

Then \(B'C'\) is easily proved equal to \(k.BC\).

And if, from \(B'\), \(C'\), parallels to \(AB\), \(AC\), be drawn, it may easily be proved that they meet on \(DA\), and that they are respectively equal to \(k.AB\), \(k.AC\).

Q. E. F.

This may be deduced from the identity

\[(a^{2}+b^{2}).(c^{2}+d^{2})=a^{2}c^{2}+b^{2}d^{2}+a^{2}d^{2}+b^{2}c^{2}.\] \[ \begin{array}{rl} (a^{2}+b^{2}).(c^{2}+d^{2}) &= a^{2}c^{2}+b^{2}d^{2}+a^{2}d^{2}+b^{2}c^{2};\\ \begin{array}{r}~\\ \text{or else}\qquad\end{array} & \left.\begin{array}{l} = a^{2}c^{2}+b^{2}d^{2}+2acbd+a^{2}d^{2}+b^{2}c^{2}-2adbc,\\ = a^{2}c^{2}+b^{2}d^{2}-2acbd+a^{2}d^{2}+b^{2}c^{2}+2adbc; \end{array}\right\}\\ \begin{array}{r}\text{i. e.}\qquad\\ \text{or else}\qquad\end{array} & \left.\begin{array}{l} = (ac+bd)^{2}+(ad-bc)^{2},\\ = (ac-bd)^{2}+(ad+bc)^{2}. \end{array}\right\} \end{array} \]

Now, if these last 2 sets are *identical*, \((ac + bd)\) must = \((ad+bc)\); for it cannot = \((ac-bd)\);

i. e., \(a(c-d)-b(c-d)\) must = 0;

i. e., \((a-b).(c-d)\) must = 0;

i. e., one or other of the first 2 sets is the sum of 2 *identical* squares.

Hence, contranominally, if *each* of the original sets consists of 2 *different* squares, their product gives the sum of 2 squares in 2 *different* ways.

Q. E. D.

Let \(BC\) be the common chord, and \(A\), \(D\), the centres.

[Figure: ]

Let \(\angle A = 30\)°, and \(\angle D = 60\)°.

And let \(BC\) (which = \(DB = DC) = 1\).

And let \(AB = x\).

Now \(\cos A = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = \dfrac{2x^{2}-1}{2x^{2}}\);

\(\therefore \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2} = 1 - \dfrac{1}{2x^{2}}\); \(\therefore \dfrac{1}{2x^{2}} = \dfrac{2-\sqrt{3}}{2}\);

\(\therefore x^{2} = \dfrac{1}{2-\sqrt{3}} = 2+\sqrt{3}\);

\(\therefore\) areas of Circles are \(\pi.(2+\sqrt{3})\) and \(\pi\);

\(\therefore\) areas of Sectors are \(\pi.\dfrac{2+\sqrt{3}}{12}\) and \(\dfrac{\pi}{6}\);

\(\therefore\) their sum \(= \pi.\dfrac{4+\sqrt{3}}{12}\).

Again, area of Triangle \(ABC = \tfrac{1}{2}.(2+\sqrt{3}).\tfrac{1}{2}\),

\(\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad= \dfrac{2+\sqrt{3}}{4}\);

also area of Triangle \(DBC = \dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4}\);

\(\therefore\) their sum \(= \dfrac{2+2\sqrt{3}}{4} = \dfrac{1+\sqrt{3}}{2}\).

Now the portion, of the smaller Circle, that is within the larger one, is the difference between these two sums;

\(\therefore\) it \(= \pi.\dfrac{4+\sqrt{3}}{12} - \dfrac{1+\sqrt{3}}{2}\).

Hence its ratio, to the area of the smaller Circle, is this sum divided by \(\pi\);

\(\therefore\) it \(= \dfrac{4+\sqrt{3}}{12} - \dfrac{1+\sqrt{3}}{2\pi}\),

\(= \dfrac{5\cdot 732}{12} - \dfrac{2\cdot 732}{(\tfrac{44}{7})} = \cdot 478 - \dfrac{\cdot 248}{(\tfrac{4}{7})}\),

\(= \cdot 478 - \dfrac{1\cdot 736}{4} = \cdot 478 - \cdot 434 = \cdot 044\).

Q. E. F.

problems.question:
In a given Triangle, whose base-angles are acute, draw two lines, at right angles to the base, and together equal to the line drawn, from the vertex, at right angles to the base, and such that

(1) they are equidistant from the line drawn from the vertex;

(2) they are equidistant from the ends of the base.

problems.solution:
Take, as unit, a side of one of the Triangles.

If the Tetrahedron be cut by a vertical Plane containing one of the slant edges, the section is a Triangle whose base is \(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\), and whose sides are \(\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{2}, 1\);

hence cosine of smaller base-angle \[ = \left(\tfrac{3}{4} + 1 - \tfrac{3}{4}\right)\cdot\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}} = \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}; \]

\(\therefore\) its sine \(= \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{3}} =\) its altitude;

and this is the altitude of the Tetrahedron;

\(\therefore\) volume of Tetrahedron \(= \dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{\sqrt{3}}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{3}}{4} = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{12}\).

Also altitude of Pyramid = altitude of Triangle whose base is \(\sqrt{2}\), and whose sides are 1, 1;

i.e. it = \(\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2}\);

\(\therefore\) volume of Pyramid = \(\dfrac{1}{3}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{2} = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{6}\).

Hence required ratio = \(\dfrac{\sqrt{2}}{6}\cdot\dfrac{12}{\sqrt{2}} = 2\).

Q. E. F.

Let \(ABC\) be the given Triangle, and \(P\) the given Point; and call its trilinear co-ordinates '\(\alpha\), \(\beta\), \(\gamma\)'.

[Figure: Three overlapping triangles sharing a central region, with outer vertices A (top), B (bottom left), C (bottom right), E (far upper right), C'' (far left), A'' (bottom center). Interior points D, U, V, Q, W, R, P, S, F, C', B', A' mark cevian and transversal intersections, forming a dense star-like projective configuration.]

From \(P\) draw \(PA′\), \(PB′\), \(PC′\), \(\perp\) the sides, and therefore equal to \(\alpha\), \(\beta\), \(\gamma\). Produce \(PA′\) and \(PC′\) to \(A''\) and \(C''\), making \(A′A'' = PA′\), and \(C′C″= PC′\). From \(C″\) draw \(C″D \perp AC\), and produce it to \(E\), making \(DE = C''D\). Join \(EA''\), cutting \(AC\) in \(R\), and \(BC\) in \(S\). Join \(C''R\), cutting \(AB\) in \(Q\). Join \(PQ\), \(PS\).

The path of the ball is evidently \(PQRSP\); and we have to calculate the length of \(AR\).

Now \(AR = DR + AD = DR + AB′ - DB′\).

First, to calculate \(DR\).

From \(P\) draw \(PU\), \(PV\), parallel to \(AB\), \(AC\); from \(C'\) draw \(C'W \perp PV\); and from \(A''\) draw \(A''F \perp AC\).

By similar Triangles, \(DR : RF \colon\colon DE : A''F \colon\colon C''D : A''F\);

\(\therefore~ DR : DF \colon\colon C''D : (C''D + A''F)\);

\(\therefore~ DR = \dfrac{DF \cdot C''D}{C''D + AF}\).

Now \(\angle C'VP = A;~ \therefore~ \angle C'PV = 90° - A\);

\(\therefore~ WP = \gamma \sin A\);

\(\therefore~ DB',~ \text{which} = 2 WP, = 2\gamma \sin A\).

Similarly, \(B'F = 2\alpha \sin C\);

\(\therefore DF = 2 (\alpha \sin C + \gamma \sin A)\).

Again, \(C'W = \gamma \cos A\);

\(\therefore~ C″D,~ \text{which}~ = 2 C'W + PB',~ = 2\gamma \cos A + \beta\).

Similarly, \(A″F = 2 \alpha \cos C + \beta\);

\(\therefore~ C''D + A″F = 2 (\alpha \cos C + \gamma \cos A + \beta)\);

\(\therefore~ DR = \dfrac{(\alpha \sin C + \gamma \sin A) \cdot (2 \gamma \cos A + \beta)}{\alpha \cos C + \gamma \cos A + \beta}\).

Now \(AB' = B'U + UA = B'U + PV\),

\(\qquad\DeclareMathOperator\cosec{cosec}\qquad\qquad=\beta \cot A + \gamma \cosec A = \dfrac{\beta \cos A + \gamma}{\sin A}\);

\(\therefore~ AB' - DB' = \dfrac{\beta \cos A + \gamma}{\sin A} - 2 \gamma \sin A\),

\(\qquad\quad\qquad\qquad= \dfrac{\beta \cos A + \gamma (1 - 2 \sin^{2} A)}{\sin A}\)

\(\qquad\quad\qquad\qquad= \dfrac{\beta \cos A + \gamma \cos 2 A}{\sin A}\).

Now \(AR = DR + AB' - DB'\);

\(\therefore~ AR = \dfrac{(\alpha \sin C + \gamma \sin A) \cdot (2 \gamma \cos A + \beta)}{\alpha \cos C + \gamma \cos A + \beta}\) \(+ \dfrac{\beta \cos A + \gamma \cos 2 A}{\sin A}\).

Q. E. F.

It is evident that Triangle \(ADE\) is similar to \(ABC\).

[Figure: A triangle with vertices A (top right), B (bottom left), C (bottom right). A horizontal transversal cuts across the interior with points D (on AB) and E (on AC), and intermediate points G and H. Points F and J lie on base BC between B and C, with segments connecting G to F and H to J, forming a trapezoid with inner subdivisions.]

Let \(\unicode{x2018}k\unicode{x2019} = \text{ratio}\, \dfrac{DE}{a} = \dfrac{AE}{b} = \dfrac{AD}{c}\).

Now \(DG = DE\); \(\therefore DG = ka\);

\(\therefore~~ GB = c - ka - kc\);

\(\therefore~~ \dfrac{GB}{c} = 1 - k - k . \dfrac{a}{c}\);

\(\therefore~~ GF~(\text{which } = GB . \dfrac{b}{c}) = b - kb - k . \dfrac{ab}{c}\);

but \(GF = DE = ka\);

\(\therefore~~ b - kb - k .\dfrac{ab}{c} = ka\);

\(\therefore~~ bc = k . (bc + ca + ab)\);

\(\therefore~~ k = \dfrac{bc}{bc + ca + ab} = \dfrac{\dfrac{1}{a}}{\dfrac{1}{a} + \dfrac{1}{b} + \dfrac{1}{c}} = \dfrac{\tfrac{1}{a}}{m}\) (say).

Hence \(AD = \dfrac{c . \dfrac{1}{a}}{m}; DG = \dfrac{1}{m} = \dfrac{c . \dfrac{1}{c}}{m}\)·

\(\therefore~~ GB~(\text{which }= c - AD - DG) = \dfrac{c . (m - \dfrac{1}{a} - \dfrac{1}{c})}{m} = \dfrac{c . \tfrac{1}{b}}{m}\);

\(\therefore~~ AD : DG : GB \colon\colon \dfrac{1}{a} : \dfrac{1}{c} : \dfrac{1}{b}\)·

Also \(DE = ka = \dfrac{1}{m} = \dfrac{1}{\dfrac{1}{a} + \dfrac{1}{b} + \dfrac{1}{c}}\)·

Q. E. F.

Draw \(BC\), \(CE\), \(BD\), equal to the given altitudes, so as to form right \(\angle\)s at \(B\) and \(C\): and produce \(DB\), \(EC\). Join \(DC\), and draw \(CF \, \perp\) to it. Join \(EB\), and draw \(BG \, \perp\) to it. With centre \(B\), and distance \(BF\), describe a circle: with centre \(C\), and distance \(CG\), describe another: let them meet at \(A\): and join \(AB\), \(AC\).

Call the altitudes of \(ABC\), '\(\alpha\), \(\beta\), \(\gamma\)'.

Now \(\alpha.BC=\beta.CA=\gamma.AB\)

= twice area of \(ABC\);

also, taking \(BC\) as unit-line,

\(BC=\dfrac{1}{BC}, \quad CA=CG=\dfrac{1}{CE}\),

\(AB=BF=\dfrac{1}{BD}\);

\(\therefore \, \dfrac{\alpha}{BC} = \dfrac{\beta}{CE} = \dfrac{\gamma}{BD}\);

i.e. \(\alpha\), \(\beta\), \(\gamma\) are proportional to given altitudes;

\(\therefore\) Triangle \(ABC\) is similar to required Triangle.

The rest of the construction is obvious.

Q. E. F.

We know that the equation

'\((a^{2} + 4b^{2} + 4c^{2}) + (4a^{2} + b^{2} + 4c^{2})\)

\(\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad\qquad+ (4a^{2} + 4b^{2} + c^{2}) = 9(a^{2} + b^{2} + c^{2})\)'

is identically true.

Hence \(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}\)

\[\begin{aligned} &= \dfrac{1}{9}·\{(a^{2}+4b^{2}+4c^{2})+(4a^{2}+b^{2}+4c^{2})+(4a^{2}+4b^{2}+c^{2})\};\\ &= \dfrac{1}{9}·\{(a^{2}+4b^{2}+4c^{2}+8bc-4ca-4ab)\\ &\quad\qquad+ (4a^{2}+b^{2}+4c^{2}-4bc+8ca-4ab)\\ &\quad\qquad+ (4a^{2}+4b^{2}+c^{2}-4bc-4ca+8ab)\};\\ &= \dfrac{1}{9}·\{(-a+2b+2c)^{2} + (2a-b+2c)^{2} + (2a+2b-c)^{2}\};\\ &= \left(\dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}\right)^{2} + \left(\dfrac{2a-b+2c}{3}\right)^{2} + \left(\dfrac{2a+2b-c}{3}\right)^{2}. \end{aligned}\]

\(\text{Now}\quad(-a+2b+2c) = 3(b+c)-(a+b+c)\);

\(\therefore\), if (\(a+b+c\)) be a multiple of 3, so also is \((-a+2b+2c)\);

\(\therefore \dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}\) is an integer;

and similarly for the other 2 fractions.

Also it may be proved that, if \(\dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}\) be equal to \(a\), or \(b\), or \(c\), then \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) can be arranged in \(A. P.\)

First, let \(\dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}=a\);

then \(-a+2b+2c=3a\); i. e. \(b+c=2a\);

secondly, let \(\dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}=b\);

then \(-a+2b+2c=3b\); i. e. \(2c=a+b\);

thirdly, let \(\dfrac{-a+2b+2c}{3}=c\);

then \(-a+2b+2c=3c\); i. e. \(2b=c+a\).

And similarly for the other 2 fractions.

Hence, contranominally, if \(a\), \(b\), \(c\) can *not* be arranged in \(A. P.\), the 2 sets of squares have no common term.

Q. E. D.

