\(1^{2}\) | \(4^{2}\) | \(4^{2}\) | \(5^{2}\) | \(2^{2}\) | \(2^{2}\) |

\(3^{2}\) | \(4^{2}\) | \(8^{2}\) | \(7^{2}\) | \(6^{2}\) | \(2^{2}\) |

\(4^{2}\) | \(5^{2}\) | \(9^{2}\) | \(8^{2}\) | \(7^{2}\) | \(3^{2}\) |

[Figure: A three-dimensional parallelepiped with outer vertices A, B, D (top face), E, F, G (bottom face). An inner rectangular box is inscribed with vertices C, J, L (upper) and O, H, K, M (lower). Multiple diagonal segments connect outer and inner vertices.]

Let each side of each Square = 2.

Then \(LG = \sqrt{3}\), \(MG = (\sqrt{2}-1)\);

\(\therefore ~ LM(=JK) = \sqrt{3-(2+1-2\sqrt{2})}\)

\(\qquad\qquad\qquad = 2^\tfrac{3}{4}\);

\(\therefore ~ OJ = OK = \dfrac{1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\).

Take \(O\) as origin, the \(X\)-axis \(\parallel\) to \(AD\), and the \(Y\)-axis to \(AB\); and let \(JK\) be part of the \(Z\)-axis.

Let equation to plane containing Triangle \(CDG\) be \[ x\cos \alpha +y\cos \beta +z\cos \gamma -p=0, \] where \(p\) is length of perpendicular dropped, from \(O\), upon this plane, and meeting it somewhere in \(LG\).

Hence we can find \(p\) from equation to \(LG\), in the \(XZ\)-plane, which will be \[ x\cos \alpha +z\cos \gamma -p=0; \] now this line contains \(L\), whose co-ordinates are \(\left(1, \dfrac{1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\right)\), and \(G\), whose co-ordinates are \(\left(\sqrt{2}, -\dfrac{1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\right)\);

\(\therefore \cos \alpha + \dfrac{1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}} \cdot \cos \gamma-p=0\),

and \(\quad\sqrt{2} \cdot \cos \alpha - \dfrac{1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}} \cdot \cos \gamma-p=0\);

\(\therefore (\sqrt{2}-1) \cdot \cos \alpha = \dfrac{2}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}} \cdot \cos \gamma = 2^\tfrac{3}{4} \cdot \cos \gamma\);

\(\therefore \dfrac{\cos \alpha}{2^\tfrac{3}{4}}=\dfrac{\cos \gamma}{\sqrt{2}-1}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{2^\tfrac{3}{2}+3-2^\tfrac{3}{2}}}=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{3}}\);

\(\therefore \cos \alpha = \dfrac{2^\tfrac{3}{4}}{\sqrt{3}},\, \cos \gamma = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}-1}{\sqrt{3}}\);

\(\therefore p = \dfrac{2^\tfrac{3}{4}}{\sqrt{3}} + \dfrac{\sqrt{2}-1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}\cdot\sqrt{3}} = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}+1}{2^\tfrac{1}{4}\cdot\sqrt{3}}\).

Now area of \(CDG = \sqrt{3}\);

\(\therefore\) volume of pyramid, whose base is \(CDG\) and whose vertex is \(O, = \dfrac{\sqrt{2}+1}{3\cdot 2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\);

and there are eight such pyramids in the solid;

\(\therefore\) their sum = \(\dfrac{8(\sqrt{2}+1)}{3 \cdot 2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\).

Also volume of pyramid, whose base is \(ABCD\), and whose vertex is \(O\), = \(\dfrac{4}{3 \cdot 2^\tfrac{1}{4}}\);

